---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ServicePageShell from '../../components/ServicePageShell.astro';
import MediaPlaceholder from '../../components/MediaPlaceholder.astro';

const chapters = [
  { id: 'daylight', label: 'Daylight, shaped around the room' },
  { id: 'window', label: 'Start with the window' },
  { id: 'privacy', label: 'Keep the view. Choose the privacy' },
  { id: 'architecture', label: 'Choose how the shade meets the architecture' },
  { id: 'coordinate', label: 'Now coordinate the room' },
  { id: 'controls', label: 'Choose the control you’ll actually use' },
  { id: 'scenes', label: 'Give each button a purpose' },
  { id: 'stage', label: 'Designed for the stage your home is in' },
  { id: 'installed', label: 'The detail, installed' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'Compare the complete scope' },
  { id: 'windows', label: 'Start with your windows' },
];
---
<Layout
  title="Motorized Shades & Lutron Shading | DAVG"
  description="Quiet motorized shading coordinated with glass, structure, lighting, and solar exposure for new and existing Denver-area homes."
>
  <ServicePageShell currentPath="/solutions/motorized-shades/" chapters={chapters} headerOffset="0px">
    <a slot="brand" class="brand-lockup" href="/">
      <span class="brand-name">DAVG</span>
      <span class="brand-est">Est. 2013</span>
    </a>

    <section id="daylight" class="band band-ink" data-rail="daylight">
      <p class="kicker">Lutron motorized shades</p>
      <h1>Daylight, shaped around the room.</h1>
      <p class="deck">Keep the view. Soften afternoon glare. Create privacy when the lights come on. DAVG designs Lutron roller shades around your windows, your rooms and the way you live.</p>
      <p class="actions"><a class="btn" href="#inquiry">Plan Your Shading</a></p>
      <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-01-room" subject="Glass-wall shading photograph" ratio="16 / 9" loading="eager" src="/images/davg-living-room-sheer-roller-shades-sun-01-1600w.webp" alt="Floor-to-ceiling sheer roller shades on a living-room glass wall, with the sun still visible through the fabric" />
      <p class="caption">Illustrative living-room glass. Product photograph, not a completed DAVG project. The same west-facing living room and a primary bedroom return through the decisions below.</p>

      <h2 class="prompt">Where is your home in the process?</h2>
      <p class="measure">These three choices describe construction conditions. Fabric, hardware and control stay available either way. Selecting one highlights the matching notes and fills the inquiry. Nothing is stored in the page address.</p>
      <div class="stage-controls" role="group" aria-label="Where is your home in the process?">
        <button type="button" data-project-stage="retrofit" aria-pressed="false">Existing home / Retrofit</button>
        <button type="button" data-project-stage="remodel" aria-pressed="false">Remodel</button>
        <button type="button" data-project-stage="custom" aria-pressed="false">Custom build</button>
      </div>
      <div class="paths paths-3">
        <article data-stage="retrofit">
          <h3>Existing home / Retrofit</h3>
          <p>Start with the windows you want to improve and the controls already installed. Fit, support, fabric, access and power come before a battery or wired recommendation.</p>
          <p>Recently purchased a home with shades? Identify the shades, processor, controls and access before replacing hardware.</p>
          <p>Preparing to hand the system to a new owner? Document the equipment, everyday controls, account access and service contact. Transfer depends on the actual system.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-stage="remodel">
          <h3>Remodel</h3>
          <p>An open ceiling or a changed window is the time to resolve layers, mounting and power while that work is still accessible.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-stage="custom">
          <h3>Custom build</h3>
          <p>Bring shading into the window and lighting plan before framing and electrical decisions are fixed.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="window" class="band band-paper" data-rail="window">
      <span id="purpose" class="alias"></span>
      <span id="openings" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Start with the window.</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>The opening sets the work: how the glass is grouped, whether someone can reach it, and how dark the room actually needs to be. A standard window, a wide glass wall, a tall opening and a bedroom do not share one size limit or one product.</p>
        <p>These four briefs use the illustrative living room and bedroom. They are not a predetermined specification, and they are not a claim that a named product fits every opening.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="example-grid">
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-02-standard" subject="Standard bedroom window" ratio="4 / 3" src="/images/davg-bedroom-motorized-shades-01-1600w.webp" alt="Bedroom with motorized roller shades at a window" />
          <figcaption>Standard window. Bedroom roller shades. Product photograph, not a DAVG project record. Group it with the room only when the hems and the daily use match.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-07-west" subject="Wide living-room glass" ratio="16 / 9" src="/images/davg-living-room-vaulted-roller-shades-01-800w.webp" alt="Vaulted living room with light roller shades on several windows and daylight still in the room" />
          <figcaption>Wide glass wall. Living-room rollers with daylight still in the room. Product photograph. Alignment across the elevation matters more than a single maximum width.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-07-glazing" subject="Tall glazed wall" ratio="16 / 9" position="center 8%" src="/images/davg-loft-tall-roller-shades-01-800w.webp" alt="Tall glazed wall with translucent roller shades closed, tree shadows falling across the fabric" />
          <figcaption>Tall opening. Roller shades closed, with tree shadows on the fabric. Product photograph. Access for service is part of the decision, not an afterthought.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-07-bedroom" subject="Bedroom window for darkness and privacy" ratio="4 / 3" src="/images/davg-triathlon-bedroom-shades-closed-01-800w.webp" alt="Bedroom with two white Triathlon shades fully closed across a wide window" />
          <figcaption>Bedroom. Triathlon shades fully closed. Product photograph. Darkness depends on the fabric and the gaps, not on the room name.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Opening</th>
              <th>Condition to settle</th>
              <th>What can change the path</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Standard window</td>
              <td>Finished size, handles, trim and whether the shade is reached for service</td>
              <td>A battery roller can fit. A pocket or a second layer needs a different assembly.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Wide glass wall</td>
              <td>How panels group, where seams fall, and how hems align</td>
              <td>One fabric width may have to become several panels. Night privacy is a separate layer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Tall or hard-to-reach opening</td>
              <td>Drive location, bracket support and a way to remove the shade later</td>
              <td>Fabric weight, roll diameter and structure can rule out a single panel.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bedroom</td>
              <td>Dawn light, night privacy and whether the room must go dark</td>
              <td>Blackout fabric alone does not make a dark room. Edge, top and bottom conditions still count.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What DAVG measures before specifying shades</h3>
      <ul class="plain-list">
        <li>Finished and rough opening dimensions at multiple points.</li>
        <li>Orientation, view priority and nighttime privacy.</li>
        <li>Mullions, handles, trim, drapery, furniture and door swing.</li>
        <li>Mounting support, power route, roll direction and service access.</li>
        <li>How neighboring shades should group, align and move.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="privacy" class="band band-forest" data-rail="privacy">
      <span id="light" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Keep the view. Choose the privacy.</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>The fabric determines what you see, how light enters and how private the room feels. Start with the job of each window, then choose one layer or two. Openness describes the open area of the weave. It is not a percentage guarantee of glare reduction, heat rejection or privacy.</p>
        <p>Use the same living room and bedroom as the example. Sample fabric in real day and night light. A shade does not reject a fixed share of UV and heat, and it does not improve HVAC efficiency by a stated amount.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="paths paths-3">
        <article>
          <h3>Keep the view</h3>
          <p>Solar-screen fabrics soften daylight while retaining a view through the weave. Openness and color change the result. More openness usually keeps more view and admits more direct light.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Create privacy</h3>
          <p>Translucent fabrics diffuse light and obscure the view. Daytime appearance does not settle nighttime privacy. Check the fabric with the interior lights on.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Darken the room</h3>
          <p>Opaque fabrics block light through the material. Bedroom darkness also depends on the gaps around the shade and the mounting detail. Room-darkening and light-tight construction are different goals.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="guide">
        <div>
          <h3>One window. Two different needs.</h3>
          <div class="measure">
            <p>The living room may need a view during the day and privacy after dark. The bedroom may need filtered daylight and a separate blackout layer for sleep. Two independently controlled fabrics can serve those moments.</p>
            <p><strong>Day.</strong> A solar layer filters daylight while keeping the exterior visible.</p>
            <p><strong>Night.</strong> A privacy or blackout layer closes the view into the room.</p>
            <p>A dual roller needs room for both assemblies. A roller paired with drapery may suit the architecture better. Palladiom and Triathlon do not have the same dual-bracket options as Sivoia. Lutron’s layering notes and room-darkening matrix are a selection vocabulary, not a performance promise for an unspecified fabric.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-gallery">
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-fabric" subject="Sheer fabric and hembar" ratio="4 / 3" position="center 58%" src="/images/davg-sivoia-sheer-hembar-detail-01-800w.webp" alt="Light Sivoia QS sheer fabric, stitched hembar, and gray Lutron end cap against a bright window" />
            <figcaption>Sivoia QS sheer. Fabric, hembar and end cap in daylight. Product photograph.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-sivoia" subject="Sheer hem with a view below" ratio="4 / 3" position="center 70%" src="/images/davg-sivoia-slate-sheer-hem-01-800w.webp" alt="Dark Sivoia QS sheer, with the hem and a narrow view of trees below the fabric" />
            <figcaption>Sivoia QS sheer, hem against the glass. Product photograph. The view through the weave is the point of this layer.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-03-day" subject="Bedroom shade partly raised" ratio="16 / 9" position="left center" src="/images/davg-palladiom-bedroom-shades-partial-01-1600w.webp" alt="Bedroom with Palladiom roller shades partly raised, daylight coming in below the fabric" />
            <figcaption>Bedroom, shade partly raised. Product photograph. Daylight below the fabric is a position, not a second fabric.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-elevation" subject="Living-room shades fully closed" ratio="16 / 9" src="/images/davg-sivoia-living-room-shades-closed-01-1600w.webp" alt="Living room with two Sivoia QS woven shades fully closed beside built-in shelves" />
            <figcaption>Living room, Sivoia QS shades fully closed. Product photograph. This is the privacy position, not proof of nighttime opacity.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="architecture" class="band band-paper" data-rail="architecture">
      <span id="selection" class="alias"></span>
      <span id="details" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Choose how the shade meets the architecture.</h2>
      <p class="measure">Recessed, fascia or intentionally exposed. The mount and the Lutron family answer the same physical question: what you see at the top of the window, and what the opening can support.</p>

      <div class="paths paths-3" id="shade-design-paths">
        <article>
          <h3>Recessed</h3>
          <p>Coordinate a ceiling pocket so the raised shade is concealed. Resolve the roller, structure, power and service access before the ceiling is finished.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Fascia</h3>
          <p>A visible cover conceals the roller without a recessed pocket. Its proportion, height and service access still matter.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Exposed</h3>
          <p>Palladiom makes the bracket and bottom rail part of the design. Choose this when the hardware should complement the window and the finishes.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h3>Three shading paths</h3>
      <p class="measure">Start with the opening and the effect you want from the fabric. Then choose the treatment, mounting detail, power and control path. These are project-fit routes, not a quality ladder and not installed prices.</p>
      <div class="paths paths-3">
        <article>
          <h3>Finished-home simplicity</h3>
          <p>A battery-powered roller approach can bring shading to selected rooms without running a new power cable to every opening.</p>
          <ul class="plain-list">
            <li>Consider Triathlon; compare Serena where its system and control options fit.</li>
            <li>Check fabric, opening size, battery access and integration.</li>
            <li>Use a mount that respects the finished room.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Integrated architectural shading</h3>
          <p>A Sivoia QS-based design can support a more coordinated shading plan, with the treatment and power path selected for the project.</p>
          <ul class="plain-list">
            <li>Coordinate rollers, drapery or other supported treatments.</li>
            <li>Plan pockets, blocking, wiring and service access.</li>
            <li>Choose compatible lighting and whole-home controls.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Exposed design detail</h3>
          <p>Palladiom roller shades make the bracket and finish part of the architecture.</p>
          <ul class="plain-list">
            <li>Choose visible hardware with intention.</li>
            <li>Select wired or wire-free equipment to suit the opening.</li>
            <li>Coordinate alignment, fabric and neighboring finishes.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="step-close">A wireless control connection does not necessarily mean battery power. Confirm communication, power and platform compatibility as separate choices.</p>

      <div class="guide">
        <div class="guide-gallery">
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-08-pocket" subject="Triathlon fascia" ratio="4 / 3" src="/images/davg-triathlon-fascia-boucle-01-800w.webp" alt="White Triathlon fascia box mounted on shiplap above a light woven shade" />
            <figcaption>Triathlon fascia above a light woven shade. Product photograph. A fascia conceals the roller without a ceiling pocket.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-04-system" subject="Roller and fabric at the ceiling" ratio="4 / 3" src="/images/davg-bedroom-shade-fascia-detail-01-800w.webp" alt="Close view of a dark roller and gray woven shade fabric at a white ceiling" />
            <figcaption>Roller and woven fabric at the ceiling. Product photograph. This shows the top condition, not a measured pocket section.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-palladiom" subject="Palladiom exposed bracket" ratio="1 / 1" position="left center" src="/images/davg-palladiom-exposed-roller-bracket-01-800w.webp" alt="Palladiom exposed roller with a fabric-wrapped tube and rounded metal bracket above a night window" />
            <figcaption>Palladiom roller hardware: fabric-wrapped tube and metal bracket. Product photograph. The keypad of the same name is a separate product.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-07-exposed" subject="Palladiom shade in a finished room" ratio="4 / 3" position="center 22%" src="/images/davg-palladiom-bathroom-window-shade-01-800w.webp" alt="Bathroom with a Palladiom shade lowered partway over a wide black-framed window" />
            <figcaption>Palladiom shade lowered over a wide window. Product photograph. Exposed hardware is a design choice, not an automatic upgrade over a concealed Sivoia shade.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Family profiles</h3>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Family</th>
                  <th>Start here when</th>
                  <th>Resolve before ordering</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Triathlon</td>
                  <td>Finished rooms and straightforward roller conditions. Battery-powered configurations can avoid a new power run. Supported wired-power configurations also exist.</td>
                  <td>Fabric, fit, top treatment and power or battery access</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Sivoia QS</td>
                  <td>Concealed pockets, layered rollers or more demanding openings. The roller follows the measured window, fabric and mounting condition.</td>
                  <td>Roller sizing, mounting, power, control and service access</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Palladiom</td>
                  <td>Visible brackets and finish coordination. Wired and Wire-Free versions are different installation paths.</td>
                  <td>Bracket support, finish, geometry and the wired or wire-free route</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="measure">A Sivoia concealed roller can be the right decision in a top-tier residence. A Palladiom Wire-Free shade can be the right retrofit. Palladiom is not the required choice for a recessed pocket.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Mounting and light-gap decisions</h3>
      <dl class="ledger">
        <div>
          <dt>Inside mount</dt>
          <dd>Preserves surrounding architecture and gives up some fabric width to brackets and operator clearance. Survey the opening at multiple points and show the expected side gap.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Outside mount</dt>
          <dd>Can overlap trim to improve coverage. The shade and brackets stay visible and may conflict with doors, handles or drapery.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Recessed pocket</dt>
          <dd>Coordinate chassis, one or two rollers, roll diameter, blocking, wire entry and a removable service opening before framing. Lutron’s measuring note gives configuration-specific minimums, including 3.5 × 3.5 inches for one Roller 64 assembly and a larger dual mount. Those figures are not a universal finished pocket.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Room darkening</dt>
          <dd>A closed fabric can still leak light at the side, top or bottom. Specify the side channel, bottom condition and pocket when darkness matters.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <details class="drawer">
        <summary>Published configuration examples</summary>
        <p>These are illustrative published configurations, not a promise that maximum width and maximum drop can occur together, and not a quote. DAVG checks the current model specification for each opening.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Published configuration</th>
                <th>Example manufacturer limit</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Triathlon Select roller</td>
                <td>Up to 108 in wide × 120 in high, subject to its available configuration</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Triathlon WIDR roller</td>
                <td>Up to 144 × 144 in in Lutron’s luxury residential matrix</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Palladiom Wire-Free roller</td>
                <td>21–144 in wide × 12–144 in high, varying by fabric</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Sivoia QS Roller 100, a specific recessed 5-in configuration</td>
                <td>A listed single-panel width range that varies by fabric and height, with a typical maximum area of 100 sq ft</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>

    <section id="coordinate" class="band band-ink" data-rail="coordinate">
      <span id="system" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Now coordinate the room.</h2>
      <p class="deck">The roller shade and the control system do different jobs. The shade manages the window. The system coordinates shades, lighting and the way you operate the home. We choose both together so the hardware, controls and everyday behavior fit the project.</p>
      <p class="measure">The shade solves the window. The control system coordinates the room.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Path</th>
              <th>Homeowner explanation</th>
              <th>Likely starting situation</th>
              <th>What must be confirmed</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Focused shade control</td>
              <td>A compatible remote or supported Lutron hub/system can manage selected shades without making a whole-home lighting project necessary.</td>
              <td>A defined room or shading-only improvement</td>
              <td>Exact shade, remote and hub compatibility, and whether grouping, an app or schedules are required</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>RadioRA 3</td>
              <td>Coordinate compatible shades and lighting through a professionally configured wireless control system, with supported keypads, remotes, app operation and scenes.</td>
              <td>Existing homes, remodels or new homes whose scope suits its supported devices and topology</td>
              <td>Shade RF path, keypad power, coverage and current supported models</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>HomeWorks</td>
              <td>Plan lighting and shading as a broader residential control architecture, including architectural keypad choices and the appropriate wired or wireless infrastructure.</td>
              <td>Projects needing its system scope, control choices or construction coordination; often custom builds and substantial remodels</td>
              <td>Processor, shade links, keypad wiring and system design. Existing-home eligibility is project-specific</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="measure">If you already have Lutron controls, we begin there. A shade addition may extend the current system. A larger project may justify a different architecture. The decision follows the home and the desired controls—not a rule that every retrofit uses RadioRA 3 or every custom build needs HomeWorks.</p>
      <p class="measure">Where the home uses Control4, compatible Lutron systems can participate in broader household scenes. Control4 is the integration layer in this explanation, not a fourth Lutron shade family. Confirm the installed processor and supported driver before promising functions.</p>
      <div class="signal" aria-label="Physical shade path and control path">
        <ol>
          <li>Fabric and roller</li>
          <li>Compatible Lutron shade and control link</li>
          <li>Selected Lutron system</li>
          <li>Keypad or app</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Optional branch: Control4 can coordinate compatible Lutron scenes. The keypad does not power the motor. Wired shade power is not the same thing as wired communication.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="controls" class="band band-paper" data-rail="controls">
      <h2>Choose the control you’ll actually use.</h2>
      <p class="measure">A shade can have its own open and close control, or join a room scene with the lights. The keypad should make those choices obvious and suit the wall where it lives. We resolve the button layout, labels and finish with the control system.</p>
      <p class="measure">That system determines which controls belong on the wall.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Control</th>
              <th>System context</th>
              <th>What to understand</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Pico remote</td>
              <td>Compatible shade or system configurations; wall, tabletop or handheld as specified</td>
              <td>Simple control without assuming every wall position needs a mains-powered keypad. The face should be the shade or scene version actually selected.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Sunnata RF keypad</td>
              <td>RadioRA 3 version reviewed for this page: 2-button, 3-button with raise/lower, and 4-button variants</td>
              <td>Fewer or more buttons change the daily task. RF communication does not make this keypad battery powered. Installation requires appropriate power. Other platforms may use a similar name; the exact model has to be identified.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>seeTouch</td>
              <td>The RadioRA 3-compatible model, and a HomeWorks model separately if that system is specified</td>
              <td>Clearly labeled button control. One photograph does not prove every seeTouch works on every system.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Palladiom keypad</td>
              <td>HomeWorks residential model</td>
              <td>Flat architectural face and coordinated finishes. A Palladiom keypad does not require Palladiom roller hardware, and the roller does not require this keypad.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Alisse</td>
              <td>HomeWorks QSX wired QS-link model</td>
              <td>Architectural metal face, circular buttons and personalized engraving. It is not a RadioRA 3 keypad upgrade.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="guide">
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-control" subject="Palladiom keypad beside a lowered shade" ratio="4 / 3" position="left center" src="/images/davg-palladiom-keypad-with-shade-01-800w.webp" alt="Brushed-metal keypad engraved Entertain, Relax, and Dine, with a lowered shade behind the seating" />
          <figcaption>Palladiom keypad, engraved Entertain, Relax and Dine, with a lowered shade beside it. Lutron product photograph, not a DAVG job record. This is the keypad, not the Palladiom roller bracket.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Proposed programming</p>
          <p class="measure">A three-scene layout might label Morning, Privacy and Evening, with raise and lower on a compatible configuration. These labels are conceptual. They are not the engraving on the photograph.</p>
          <ol class="sequence">
            <li>Morning — raise the living-room solar shade.</li>
            <li>Privacy — lower the bedroom privacy layer.</li>
            <li>Evening — close the selected shades with the lighting scene.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="example-grid pending-grid">
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-pico" subject="Pico shade remote, photograph pending" ratio="4 / 3" />
          <figcaption>Pico shade or scene remote. Photograph pending. No stand-in image is shown.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-06-sunnata" subject="Sunnata RadioRA 3 keypad layouts, photographs pending" ratio="4 / 3" />
