    <section id="scenes" class="band band-forest band-solar" data-rail="scenes">
      <span id="solar" class="alias"></span>
      <p class="spatial-word" aria-hidden="true">SHADES</p>
      <h2>Give each button a purpose.</h2>
      <p class="deck">Return to the same two rooms. These are proposed responses for the illustrative living room and bedroom, not a recorded DAVG program and not a sun-tracking simulation.</p>
      <div class="time-controls" role="group" aria-label="Proposed time of day">
        <button type="button" data-scene="Morning" aria-pressed="true">Morning</button>
        <button type="button" data-scene="Afternoon" aria-pressed="false">Afternoon</button>
        <button type="button" data-scene="Evening" aria-pressed="false">Evening</button>
      </div>
      <p class="caption" id="scene-caption"><span id="scene-label">Morning</span>. <span id="scene-copy">Living room: raise the solar shade and let the view in. Bedroom: open enough for daylight without a separate task at the glass.</span></p>
      <div class="example-grid">
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-07-living-scene" subject="Illustrative living room" ratio="16 / 9" src="/images/davg-living-room-lutron-shades-orlando-01-1600w.webp" alt="Living room with roller shades and daylight in the room" />
          <figcaption>Living room used as the scene example. Product photograph. One picture stands in for the written scene. It does not change with the buttons.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-05-solar" subject="Bedroom window wall at mixed shade positions" ratio="16 / 9" src="/images/davg-bedroom-window-wall-roller-shades-01-1600w.webp" alt="Bedroom window wall with black roller shades held at different heights above a view of trees" />
          <figcaption>Bedroom window wall, shades at different heights. Product photograph. The heights illustrate positions. They are not a validated afternoon glare study.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>Operate one window, a room or a group. Use a compatible keypad, remote or app, and set schedules around the household. Where the selected system supports it, Lutron’s Natural Light Optimization can adjust shade positions around the sun’s movement. That feature is specified per system. It is not assumed here.</p>
        <p>For a home with Control4, compatible Lutron shading can join broader household scenes after the processor, integration and intended behavior are confirmed.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="stage" class="band band-paper" data-rail="stage">
      <span id="construction" class="alias"></span>
      <span id="pitfalls" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Designed for the stage your home is in.</h2>
      <p class="measure">The same daylight goal can require very different work in a finished room and a home still on paper. We plan the shade around the construction opportunity, then coordinate the details with the people responsible for the window, ceiling and controls.</p>
      <div class="stage-controls" role="group" aria-label="Project stage, repeated from the introduction">
        <button type="button" data-project-stage="retrofit" aria-pressed="false">Existing home / Retrofit</button>
        <button type="button" data-project-stage="remodel" aria-pressed="false">Remodel</button>
        <button type="button" data-project-stage="custom" aria-pressed="false">Custom build</button>
      </div>
      <div class="paths paths-3">
        <article data-stage="retrofit">
          <h3>Existing home / Retrofit</h3>
          <p>Start with the windows you want to improve and the controls already installed. We assess fit, support, fabric, access and available power before recommending battery or wired options.</p>
          <p>Coordinate existing trim, mounting support, finish protection, access and any targeted electrical work. A battery shade, exposed hardware or fascia may mean less construction. It still needs support and a service path.</p>
          <p>Recently purchased a home? Identify the existing shades, processor, controls and access arrangements before replacing hardware. Explain what can be retained and what needs service or reconfiguration.</p>
          <p>Preparing a handoff? Document the equipment, everyday controls, applicable account ownership and service contact. Coordinate transfer and remove former-owner access as appropriate. Do not assume every platform transfers automatically.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-stage="remodel">
          <h3>Remodel</h3>
          <p>An open ceiling or changed window is an opportunity to resolve a cleaner detail. Decide layers, mounting and power while the affected work is accessible.</p>
          <p>Coordinate revised elevations, pocket sections, blocking, power routing, finish interfaces and a service opening.</p>
        </article>
        <article data-stage="custom">
          <h3>Custom build</h3>
          <p>Bring shading into the window and lighting plan early. Coordinate each opening’s fabric, assembly and controls before framing and electrical decisions are fixed.</p>
          <p>Share the opening schedule, glazing and elevations, pocket or roller sections, support, electrical responsibilities, keypad locations and the finish or engraving schedule.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <h3>For architects, builders and interior designers</h3>
      <p class="measure">We coordinate the technology portion with the architectural and interior design intent: shade assemblies, power and control requirements, proposed keypad locations and the interfaces that need agreement. Structural, architectural and electrical work stays with the appropriate project team. DAVG is not presented here as an architecture practice.</p>
      <h3>Questions to settle in the proposal</h3>
      <ol class="plain-list">
        <li>What framing, finish or electrical work is required at each opening?</li>
        <li>Who provides bracket blocking, power and the final pocket or fascia dimensions?</li>
        <li>Can a shade be removed for service without damaging the finish?</li>
        <li>When is the opening field measured, and who approves fabric, seam and hembar alignment?</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Problems worth preventing</h3>
      <dl class="ledger">
        <div>
          <dt>No solid mounting structure</dt>
          <dd>Issue blocking details before the relevant walls or ceilings close.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Pocket sized by guess</dt>
          <dd>Size it from the selected shade, width, fabric roll and configuration.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Misaligned hems across glass</dt>
          <dd>Survey and group the elevation as one composition.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Late decision</dt>
          <dd>Deciding after the ceiling is closed can force a visible fascia, a light gap, or the loss of a second layer. That is a construction consequence, not a published price.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <figure>
        <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-08-cutaway" subject="Architectural pocket cutaway" ratio="16 / 9" />
        <figcaption>Pocket section drawing pending. The fascia and bracket photographs above are finished top conditions, not a construction section and not a DAVG project record.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="installed" class="band band-forest" data-rail="installed">
      <span id="proof" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>The detail, installed.</h2>
      <p class="measure">A verified DAVG project is not published here yet. This slot stays empty until a project record can name the window, the shading objective and the coordination. Product photographs elsewhere on this page are not that record. Comfort and energy results are omitted until they are measured.</p>
      <MediaPlaceholder id="shading-11-proof" subject="Reserved shading project photograph" ratio="16 / 9" />
    </section>

    <section id="scope" class="band band-paper" data-rail="scope">
      <span id="investment" class="alias"></span>
      <span id="ownership" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Compare the complete scope.</h2>
      <p class="measure">The investment changes with the openings, the fabric and layers, the mount and shade family, the control system, the keypad and the construction. Good, Better and Best describe DAVG project scope. They are not Lutron grades, not a Triathlon-to-Palladiom quality ladder, and not a rule that RadioRA 3 or HomeWorks occupies a fixed tier. No installed prices are published.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Scope</th>
              <th>What it delivers</th>
              <th>What the scope includes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Good — focused shading</td>
              <td>Solve a defined glare or privacy problem in selected rooms</td>
              <td>A suitable fabric and layer, a mount that fits the finished opening, and straightforward shade control</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Better — coordinated rooms</td>
              <td>Consistent shading across related rooms, with useful groups and schedules</td>
              <td>More openings, finish coordination, and a keypad, remote or system plan such as RadioRA 3 when that system fits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Best — architectural shading</td>
              <td>Resolve shading with the ceiling, lighting and household operation</td>
              <td>Selected pockets or exposed finishes, a second layer where it is justified, a broader system such as HomeWorks when the project needs it, keypad finish and greater trade coordination</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="measure">Any shade family can appear in more than one scope. A whole-home Triathlon project may cost more than a single Palladiom opening. A proposal should list, opening by opening, the drive, fabric, layers, mount, power, control, light-gap expectation, service method and construction allowance. Generic per-window pricing is not published.</p>
      <h3>Delivery</h3>
      <ol class="sequence">
        <li>Measure and sample.</li>
        <li>Specify each opening.</li>
        <li>Coordinate mounting and power.</li>
        <li>Install and program.</li>
        <li>Demonstrate controls and service access.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="measure">DAVG is a Lutron Certified HomeWorks Integrator. The work is to verify platform, shade and control compatibility; survey and measure each opening; present fabric and hardware samples; create the shade, power, control and pocket schedules; coordinate the related trades; order the approved configuration; program grouping, scenes and limits; and document maintenance access. Lutron is selected when its shade families, hardware, controls and service path meet the brief.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="windows" class="band band-paper" data-rail="windows">
      <span id="questions" class="alias"></span>
      <h2>Start with your windows.</h2>
      <div class="measure">
        <p>Tell us which rooms need better light or privacy, whether the home is finished or under construction, and what controls are already installed. Plans or window photographs help define the next step.</p>
        <p>At elevation, strong sun and large temperature swings make west- and south-facing glass demanding. A Denver-area shade plan should account for seasonal sun angle, the view, privacy after dark, and high-contrast daylight on screens and materials.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Can shades be added without opening the walls?</summary>
          <p>Often. Battery-powered configurations can avoid a new power run, but mounting support, dimensions and access still need checking.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do solar shades provide privacy at night?</summary>
          <p>Do not assume so. When the room is brighter than outdoors, an open-weave fabric can allow a view inside. Test the fabric at the real window and add a privacy or blackout layer where evening privacy is required.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does blackout fabric make a bedroom completely dark?</summary>
          <p>It blocks light through the fabric. Edge gaps, the top detail and the bottom condition also determine the room’s darkness.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Are wireless shades always battery powered?</summary>
          <p>No. Wireless describes the control link. Some shades use wired power with wireless communication. Battery models need service access and eventual replacement. Wired-power models need a planned power path.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which Lutron shade is best?</summary>
          <p>The fit depends on the opening and the architectural goal. Triathlon, Sivoia QS and Palladiom serve different installation and design priorities. There is no single maximum size. Published examples range from Triathlon Select configurations up to 108 × 120 inches to larger specified Palladiom and Sivoia configurations. Fabric, width, drop, area, seam, drive and mounting still have to pass the current sizing tool.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can they work with my existing controls?</summary>
          <p>We check the existing Lutron or other automation system, processor and integration before confirming compatibility. Compatible shades and lighting can share a programmed scene. A local override stays available, and a schedule should be explainable.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much pocket depth do motorized shades actually need?</summary>
          <p>There is no universal depth. For illustration, Lutron’s measuring guide identifies 3.5 × 3.5 inches as minimum mounting space for one Roller 64 assembly and 7.5 inches of depth for a particular dual Roller 64 assembly. That does not determine the finished pocket for another roller, fabric, fascia or service detail. Size the selected assembly on a section before framing.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the structural requirement on a large window wall?</summary>
          <p>The bracket, fastener and substrate must support the selected shade and its operating load. Blocking or another approved attachment is detailed with the architect or builder, particularly where drywall will conceal the support. The measured opening and the installation instructions control the answer.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

